# ── docker/Caddyfile ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Trusted HTTPS for your stack using certificates issued by your OWN Tailscale tailnet.
# No Let's Encrypt, no DNS challenges, no self-signed warnings, nothing exposed to the internet.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
#   Caddy 2.5+ detects any site address ending in .ts.net and fetches the TLS certificate at
#   handshake time from the local tailscaled (via the socket mounted in docker-compose.yml).
#   Certs auto-renew. The SAME url works on your LAN and remotely — anywhere the client is on
#   Tailscale — because MagicDNS resolves the name to the tailnet IP either way.
#
# PREREQUISITES (once)
#   1. Admin console → DNS → enable MagicDNS AND "Enable HTTPS Certificates".
#      (Your rig being on Tailscale is NOT enough — cert issuance is a separate toggle.)
#   2. Get this box's MagicDNS name:   tailscale status        # e.g. cachybox.tailnet-abcd.ts.net
#      Put it in docker/.env as TS_HOSTNAME.
#   3. The compose file mounts the tailscaled socket and Caddy runs as root in the container,
#      so no extra permission setup is needed. (If you switch Caddy to non-root, grant its UID
#      access via TS_PERMIT_CERT_UID on the host — see README.)

# ── Main entrypoint: Open WebUI over trusted HTTPS ─────────────────────────────
# → https://<your-host>.ts.net
{$TS_HOSTNAME} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy open-webui:8080
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — put the APIs behind HTTPS too, on the same hostname, different ports.
# Uncomment the blocks you want, and uncomment the matching published ports in
# docker-compose.yml (caddy service).
#
# NOTE: Traffic over Tailscale is ALREADY encrypted (WireGuard). Plain-HTTP access to these
# services on their normal ports (:4000, :8000, :11434) is perfectly safe over the tailnet.
# Only do this if a specific client insists on an https:// URL (e.g. a browser secure-context
# feature). If Caddy fails to get a cert for a port-suffixed block on your tailnet, just drop
# back to the plain-HTTP ports — no loss of security.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# LiteLLM gateway  →  https://<host>.ts.net:8443/v1
# {$TS_HOSTNAME}:8443 {
# 	reverse_proxy litellm:4000
# }

# mcpo tools  →  https://<host>.ts.net:8444/docs
# {$TS_HOSTNAME}:8444 {
# 	reverse_proxy mcpo:8000
# }

# Ollama (native on host)  →  https://<host>.ts.net:8445/v1
# {$TS_HOSTNAME}:8445 {
# 	reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:11434
# }

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ALTERNATIVE — LAN-only friendly names for clients that are NOT on Tailscale.
# Uses Caddy's built-in local CA; clients must trust Caddy's root cert once
# (`docker exec caddy caddy trust`, or import /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt).
# Point a .home name at the server IP via your router DNS / Pi-hole / hosts file.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# chat.home {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy open-webui:8080
# }
# llm.home {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy litellm:4000
# }
